320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E ( As with so many areas artificial intelligence has broken into in the past few years, none feel more personal than the new frontier AI is reaching today: that of the human skull. \). Since their creation in the 1990s as experimental tools for severely paralyzed patients, BCIs have transformed into what has essentially become an engineering race, with a combination of invasive and non-invasive devices performing the difficult task of accurately translating neural signals into intent with the speed and precision we would only expect out of science fiction a decade ago.  [1])
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 161 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [1393 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 161 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E ( A BCI is fundamentally engineered toward the exact same challenge of transforming a chaotic, multi-dimensional signal into a coherent, precise command. Invasive BCI use devices, called electrode arrays, which are placed directly on or even deep within the brain, to gain highly detailed access to neural cell activity. Companies such as Neuralink, and research projects such as BrainGate, use this sort of approach, where a greater deal of control over neural activity can be acquired. However, invasive BCI can be very expensive and risky due to the surgery required to install electrodes in or on the brain, and also dueto concerns of long-term biocompatibility as the devices exist within the body. The most common non-invasive BCI rely on EEG headsets, which record electrical brain activity through the scalp.  [1][2][3])
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 161 0 R
/T (Ethically Troubling Territory)
/E (Ethically Troubling Territory)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1394 0 R
/E (Neural Data Has Few Analogues as Far as Sensitivity Data Is Concerned)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E ( The unique properties of data generated from an individual's brain are truly second-to-none when one considers personal data, given that these signals have been demonstrated to carry with them information beyond a person's intentional selection. [4])
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (   2. Agency And Authenticity)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E ( It has been stated by numerous proponents of BCI technology that what they are capable of doing is more often predicting an intent than actually reading a decoded output.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1395 0 R
/E ( Dual-Use, coercion risks And "Brainjacking")
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E ( The same decoded neural signals that enable the formerly paralyzed to communicate and control complex apparatus could also be used maliciously for surveillance purposes, including coercive interrogation. It has already been empirically proven that 'brainjacking' is a very real concern; hackers can infiltrate a BCI system remotely, without the consent and knowledge of the user, and gain detailed insights into his thoughts,)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
