392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E ( Artificial intelligence is getting very good at making decisions. But as these systems become more powerful, one question becomes harder to ignore: how do we know why an AI made a particular decision? A model can approve a loan, flag a medical image, or reject an application without giving a reason a person can understand. [3][4])
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E ( That is the problem Explainable AI, or XAI, tries to address. It is not about making every model completely transparent. It is about giving people useful ways to understand, question, and evaluate what a model is doing. [3][4])
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 165 0 R
/T (Explainable AI \(X-AI\): Opening the Black Box of Machine Learning )
/E (Explainable AI \(X-AI\): Opening the Black Box of Machine Learning )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 165 0 R
/T (Abstract)
/E (Abstract)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 165 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E ( Modern machine-learning systems can contain millions or even billions of learned parameters. They can recognize patterns that are difficult for a human to describe, but that same complexity makes their decisions difficult to trace. This is the black-box problem. [3][4])
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E ( It matters most when AI is used where a wrong decision has real consequences. In healthcare, finance, hiring, and other high-stakes settings, accuracy alone is not always enough. People need to know whether an output makes sense and whether the system is relying on the right information. [3][4])
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 165 0 R
/Pg 20 0 R
/A [1415 0 R 0]
/K [13]
>>
