421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E ( In healthcare, explainability can help clinicians examine why an AI system flagged a scan or predicted a particular risk. In finance, it can help explain credit decisions. In hiring, it can expose whether a system is relying on inappropriate patterns. The goal is not to replace human judgment, but to help people evaluate the machine's output. [3][4][6])
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E ( Explainability is also useful during development. When engineers can see what a model is responding to, they can identify unexpected correlations and investigate errors before deployment. XAI can therefore serve both users and developers. [1][2][3])
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E ( The biggest trade-off is accuracy versus interpretability. Simpler models are often easier to understand, while highly complex models can capture patterns that simpler systems miss. [3][4])
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [12]
>>
