459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (Affective Computing is a branch of science that aims to enable computers to recognise, understand and react to emotions of people. Combining various signals such as facial expressions, voice, language and physiological information Affective Computing attempts to improve interaction between humans and computers. However emotions are complex entities. Influenced by many factors including culture and context making inference about emotions is not an easy task. The article examines mechanisms of emotion recognition and discusses possible issues regarding accuracy, consent, privacy and behavioural targeting. Also some potential uses of Affective Computing are examined along with possible ethical guidelines. It is important to create emotion-aware technology respecting human autonomy and privacy.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
