605 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (TinyML brings machine learning directly to small, low-power devices such as microcontrollers and sensors. Instead of sending every piece of data to the cloud, these systems can process information locally, enabling faster decisions, lower bandwidth use, and better privacy. This article explores how TinyML works, where it is being used, the tools that support it, and the challenges involved in bringing intelligent models onto hardware with very limited resources. [1][3])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 175 0 R
/Pg 30 0 R
/A [1472 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 175 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E ( AI and machine learning have transformed areas ranging from healthcare and agriculture to manufacturing and transportation. Much of this intelligence, however, still depends on cloud servers and a reliable internet connection. That can introduce latency, consume bandwidth, increase costs, and raise privacy concerns when sensitive data has to leave the device. [1][3])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
609 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E ( TinyML approaches the problem from the other direction: instead of moving data to powerful machines, it brings a small amount of intelligence to the device itself. A smartwatch, environmental sensor, or industrial controller can run an ML model locally, making TinyML useful where power, connectivity, and physical space are limited. [1][3])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 175 0 R
/T (Understanding TinyML)
/E (Understanding TinyML)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E ( TinyML is more than simply shrinking a machine-learning model. It is an entire workflow built around extremely limited hardware. Models are normally trained on a PC or cloud environment and then optimized before being deployed on microcontrollers that may have only kilobytes of RAM and modest processing power. [1])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E ( The field brings together embedded systems, machine learning, model compression, and hardware-aware optimization. The result is AI designed to work within the constraints of the device rather than expecting the device to provide cloud-level resources. [1][2])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
613 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E ( The process begins much like conventional machine learning: a model is trained on a large dataset using powerful hardware. Once it performs well enough, it has to be made small and efficient enough to run on the target device. [1][2])
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
