/Pg 33 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
670 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E ( Modern computing infrastructure operates at a huge scale. Data centers already consume a significant share of global electricity, while demand from AI and other computation-heavy applications continues to grow [2]. Even though processors and hardware are becoming more efficient, the amount of computation we ask them to perform is growing just as quickly, or faster.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
671 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E ( That means improving individual hardware components is not enough. We also need to ask whether the algorithms themselves are doing more work than necessary. Energy and carbon cost can become another design constraint, alongside time, memory, and accuracy [1][2].)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
672 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E ( Climate-aware computing means including environmental impact when making technical decisions. A developer might choose a smaller model, reduce unnecessary data movement, use fewer computation cycles, or schedule workloads when cleaner energy is available [1][2].)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
673 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E ( The idea is not to make every system less capable. It is to find cases where a little less computation can provide almost the same result. If two approaches solve the same problem, but one uses considerably fewer resources, there is a good reason to consider the more efficient option [3][4].)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
674 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 178 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
675 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 178 0 R
/T (Abstract)
/E (Abstract)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Computing is getting more powerful, but it is also using more energy. As data centers, AI systems, and digital services continue to grow, their environmental impact is becoming harder to ignore [1][2]. Climate-conscious computing asks a simple question: can we design algorithms that are not only fast and accurate, but also more responsible with energy and resources? This article looks at practical ways to make computing more sustainable and why that matters.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 178 0 R
/Pg 33 0 R
/A [1488 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
