701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 179 0 R
/T (Case Study: Google Data Centers)
/E (Case Study: Google Data Centers)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
702 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E ( One well-known example comes from DeepMind's work with Google on data-center cooling. An AI-based system used information such as sensor readings, weather conditions, and expected workloads to recommend changes to cooling operations [3][4].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
703 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E ( The example shows that algorithms designed around changing conditions can reduce resource use without simply reducing performance. Instead of treating energy consumption as fixed, the system used prediction and adaptive control to make the infrastructure itself more efficient [3][4].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
704 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 179 0 R
/T (Challenges, Responsibility & Future Directions)
/E (Challenges, Responsibility & Future Directions)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
705 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E ( Sustainability is not only an engineering problem. Decisions about model size, training methods, hardware, and deployment all have consequences beyond the computer itself. Environmental impact therefore belongs alongside other responsible-AI concerns such as fairness, safety, and transparency [1][3].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
706 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E ( This does not mean choosing the smallest or least powerful solution every time. The better question is whether the extra computation is actually providing enough value to justify its cost. Making that trade-off visible is an important part of responsible design [1][4].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
707 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 179 0 R
/Pg 34 0 R
/A [1497 0 R 0]
/K [18]
>>
endobj
708 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 179 0 R
/Pg 34 0 R
/A [1498 0 R 0]
/K [19]
>>
endobj
709 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 179 0 R
/Pg 34 0 R
/A [1499 0 R 0]
/K [20]
>>
endobj
710 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 180 0 R
/E ( Climate-conscious computing asks us to expand the way we think about efficiency. Speed, accuracy, and scalability still matter, but so do energy use and environmental impact. By reducing unnecessary computation and data movement, choosing appropriate models, and considering sustainability throughout a system's lifecycle, developers can make computing more responsible without giving up its benefits [1][2][3].)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
711 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 180 0 R
/E (The goal is not to slow technological progress. It is to make that progress more thoughtful. As digital systems become a larger part of everyday life, designing them with the planet in mind may become just as important as designing them for the people who use them [3][4].)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
